module souther.time

// A local time of day (ADR-0028, [#stdlib-time]). `Time` is `java.time.LocalTime` held to the
// second: what a `DateTime` carries beside its `Date` ([#stdlib-datetime]), and what a model naming
// an opening hour holds on its own, where writing a `DateTime` would invent a day the domain does
// not have.
//
// `fromParts` reads three Ints as a time of day and answers `NotATime` where they name none — hour
// 24, minute 60, the leap second. The parts are values rather than text, so the refusal is a
// business result at the position that built it, not a compile error ([#temporal-literal] is where
// a written time is refused at compile time instead). This is the same shape `String.toInt` has,
// and for the same reason: what a caller may hand over is not known until it runs.
//
//     Time.fromParts(Time.hour(t), Time.minute(t), Time.second(t)) == t
//
// holds for every `Time`, which is what makes this the inverse of the readers rather than a second
// way in that answers something else.
let fromParts (hour: Int, minute: Int, second: Int): Time | NotATime = intrinsic "time.fromParts"

// The parts of a time, as `Date.year` / `month` / `day` are the parts of a date ([#stdlib-date]).
// All three are Ints: the hour 0 to 23, the minute and the second 0 to 59.
let hour (t: Time): Int = intrinsic "time.hour"
let minute (t: Time): Int = intrinsic "time.minute"
let second (t: Time): Int = intrinsic "time.second"
// There is no time arithmetic here. Adding to a time of day has to say what happens at midnight —
// whether 23:30 plus an hour is 00:30 or is nothing — and that is a decision about the domain
// rather than about the clock. A model that needs it holds a `DateTime` and adds there, where the
// day is present to carry the answer.
